<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/expert/global-governance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Global governance is <strong>the management of shared problems without a world
    government</strong>: institutions, treaties, norms and networks coordinating sovereign states
    that answer to no higher power. Its central tension is structural — effectiveness wants
    binding commitment, sovereignty forbids it — and everything in the field is a workaround for
    that tension. This chapter reads the unit's six topics through the lens: how globalisation
    transformed the state, what the political and economic architecture actually does, the human
    rights and environmental regimes, the distribution of power and development, regionalism's
    deepest case (the EU), and the grand theories that explain why all of it looks different
    depending on which you bring.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the architecture, the assessment, the climate test, the current stress" widget="equationStep" config={globgovCfg} />

  <h2>The state and globalisation: transformed, not dissolved</h2>
  <p>
    Globalisation — the deepening of cross-border flows of goods, capital, people, data and
    pollution — reshaped sovereignty without abolishing it. The measured changes: world trade
    (imports plus exports) runs at roughly 60% of global GDP, against about 20% a century ago;
    capital moves at screen speed; and supply chains fragment production across jurisdictions so
    thoroughly that a single product may cross borders half a dozen times before sale. Three
    scholarly positions frame the argument: hyperglobalisers claimed the state was finished
    (Ohmae's borderless world); sceptics showed globalisation is thinner than the 1890s peak and
    states remain the decisive units; transformationalists (Held and colleagues) split the
    difference — a new, deeper configuration in which states adapt rather than vanish. The
    pandemic was the arbiter in one respect: borders, export bans and fiscal rescue packages
    returned with force. The state is not dead; it is embedded — taxed, constrained and networked
    by what crosses it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "globalisation and sovereignty are simply opposites — states
    must choose between openness and control." The relation is restructuring, not subtraction:
    the same decades that raised trade shares also multiplied border agencies, capital-scan
    systems, export controls and sanctions regimes. States deliberately pooled sovereignty
    (tariff-setting to the WTO, monetary policy to a common central bank) precisely to regain
    control over what unilateral action could no longer deliver — the analytical mistake is
    treating pooled sovereignty as lost sovereignty rather than sovereignty re-instrumented.
  </p>

  <h2>Political and economic governance: the machinery, honestly assessed</h2>
  <p>
    The political layer is the UN (1945): a Security Council whose five permanent members hold
    vetoes — WWII's winners built into the architecture, paralysis by design wherever great-power
    interests clash (Russia's vetoes on Syria; the US's on Israel-related resolutions) — above a
    General Assembly, and beneath both a lattice of specialised agencies (WHO, UNICEF, IAEA) that
    deliver daily value without making headlines. The economic layer is Bretton Woods (1944): the
    IMF (balance-of-payments lender, with conditions attached and a weighted vote — the US's
    roughly 17% share plus an 85% supermajority rule gives it a veto), the World Bank (development
    finance), and the trade regime (GATT, the WTO from 1995), whose dispute-settlement system was
    the closest thing to binding adjudication in the system — until the US blocked judicial
    appointments and paralysed the Appellate Body from December 2019. The pattern to teach: the
    machinery works when great powers want it to, and the current era's stress test is precisely
    that they increasingly don't.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the UN is a world government." It has no taxing power, no
    standing army, and its "binding" resolutions require member states to enforce them. The UN
    system is a coordination and legitimation platform for sovereign states — which is why it
    succeeds at technical coordination (vaccines, standards, peacekeeping logistics) and fails
    exactly where enforcement meets a permanent member's veto.
  </p>

  <h2>Human rights and environmental governance: norms against sovereignty</h2>
  <p>
    The human rights regime is the boldest normative claim in world politics: the Universal
    Declaration (1948), the twin covenants (1966), regional courts (the European Court of Human
    Rights being the strongest, its judgments binding on 46 states) and the International
    Criminal Court (Rome Statute, 1998; 124 states parties) — with the great absentees (the US,
    China, India, Russia) marking the sovereignty line. Enforcement is the open wound: treaty
    bodies monitor, courts bind their joiners, and atrocities proceed when the powerful are
    parties to the dispute.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the International Criminal Court prosecutes heads of state,
    so sovereignty has been tamed." The docket corrects the impression: the court's early
    caseload concentrated overwhelmingly on African situations, and even the 2023 arrest
    warrant for a sitting permanent-member head of state (Russia's president, over the
    deportation of Ukrainian children) proceeds nowhere while he remains unarrested — the court
    binds those within its reach and cannot compel the powerful. Universal norms, selective
    reach: that asymmetry is the regime's defining empirical fact, not an accident of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The environmental regime shows the institutional learning curve in
    two treaties: Kyoto (1997) imposed binding targets on developed states — and the US never
    ratified, Canada withdrew (2011); Paris (2015) inverted the design — voluntary nationally
    determined contributions, procedurally mandatory review and five-yearly ratchet. Weaker law,
    wider buy-in: Paris has near-universal membership. The success story hides in between: the
    Montreal Protocol (1987) on ozone-depleting substances achieved universal ratification and is
    measurably healing the ozone layer — proof that the collective-action problem is solvable
    when costs are low, alternatives exist and monitoring is cheap.
  </p>

  <h2>Power and development: who runs the system, and for whom</h2>
  <p>
    Power analysis splits into currencies: hard (coercion, payment) and soft (attraction,
    agenda-setting — Nye's term), structural (shaping what others want) and the emerging
    currency of standards and data. The distribution is shifting: the "unipolar moment" after
    1991 gave way to multipolar rivalry, and institutions built in 1944–45 now under-represent
    the economies they coordinate — the G7's share of world GDP has fallen from about two-thirds
    to well under half, while the G20 (elevated after the 2008 crisis) and an expanded BRICS
    (Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran and the UAE joining in 2024) formalise the adjustment. Development
    governance follows the same contested map: the SDGs (2015, 17 goals) replaced the MDGs;
    aid flows run in the tens of billions annually against remittances that now exceed official
    aid to low- and middle-income countries by a wide margin; and debt distress is the current
    crisis. The exam-ready formulation: the governance system still encodes 1945's power map —
    and legitimacy is leaking exactly along that discrepancy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "international law is worthless because nobody enforces it."
    Compliance is actually high day to day — mail crosses borders, planes land, tariffs are paid,
    treaties are reported against — because states care about reputation, reciprocity and
    domestic ratification costs. Enforcement is scarce at the top of the power distribution;
    below it, institutions quietly coordinate most interactions most of the time (Keohane's
    finding in <em>After Hegemony</em>: cooperation survives hegemonic decline when institutions
    lower the cost of trust).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The UN's quiet output.</strong> Beneath the veto headlines runs a working system:
    peacekeeping (a dozen-odd operations, some 70,000 personnel, at a fraction of one percent
    of world military spending — with a measurable record: peacekeeping deployments
    statistically reduce the risk of war recurrence); WHO's eradication and epidemic
    machinery; the IAEA's inspections (the system that found Iraq's programme absent in 2003
    and monitors Iran's); and the treaty-reporting lattice that makes state behaviour
    comparable and comparable behaviour criticisable. The analytical habit the topic trains:
    assess institutions by their routine outputs, not by their loudest failures — the UN is
    simultaneously the Security Council's paralysis and the smallpox eradication.
  </p>

  <h2>Regionalism and the European Union: the deepest experiment</h2>
  <p>
    Regionalism — governance at intermediate scale — ranges from free trade areas (NAFTA/USMCA)
    through customs unions to the EU, the deepest integration ever attempted: 27 states, a
    single market (1993) with four freedoms (goods, services, capital, people), a common currency
    among 20 members, a parliament, a court with primacy over national law (<em>Van Gend en
    Loos</em>, 1963, established direct effect; <em>Costa v ENEL</em>, 1964, primacy), and a
    regulatory state whose standards propagate worldwide — the "Brussels effect" (Bradford):
    because the EU market is huge, its rules on data (GDPR), chemicals and product safety become
    de facto global standards. The EU is also the discipline's standing experiment in pooled
    sovereignty: member states remain the treaties' masters, yet several policy areas run on
    qualified majority voting, which removes national vetoes. The eurozone crisis (2010–2015)
    showed the design's friction: monetary policy pooled, fiscal policy national — so the system
    improvised bailout machinery (the troika programmes for Greece, Ireland and Portugal) that
    pooled more sovereignty in practice than any treaty had planned. Brexit (voted 2016, exit 2020) is
    the natural experiment's data point: exit is possible, costly, and did not trigger
    dissolution — the union is neither a superstate nor a treaty club but a novel, contested
    middle.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparative theories: three lenses on one system</h2>
  <p>
    The grand theories disagree about what global governance fundamentally is. Realism: anarchy
    persists, states maximise relative power, and institutions are tools of the powerful — it
    explains the Security Council's structure and why climate burden-sharing stalls on relative
    gains. Liberal institutionalism: institutions matter because they reduce transaction costs,
    lengthen the shadow of the future and create iterate cooperation — it explains the WTO's
    heyday, Montreal's success and Paris's ratchet. Constructivism: interests themselves are
    made by norms ("anarchy is what states make of it" — Wendt, 1992), so human rights and
    environmental norms can reconstitute what sovereignty means — it explains why decolonisation,
    the anti-landmine campaign (Ottawa Treaty, 1997, driven by civil society) and climate
    framing moved states that power alone did not. Marxist/world-systems readings (Wallerstein's
    core–periphery) explain the system's distributional skew — who writes the rules, who bears
    adjustment. Each lens owns a region of the evidence; the analytical discipline is knowing
    which claim each is competent to test.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Paris's arithmetic, honestly read.</strong> The agreement's design is procedural
    (voluntary pledges, five-yearly stocktakes), and the UNEP Emissions Gap reports translate
    the pledges: current NDCs leave the world on track for roughly 2.5–2.9°C of warming this
    century against the 1.5–2°C targets — a shortfall the ratchet mechanism is meant to close,
    cycle by cycle. The design wager: transparency, peer pressure and falling technology
    costs (solar, batteries) do the work enforcement treaties used to attempt. Kyoto's
    contrasting fate (binding targets, US non-ratification, Canada's 2011 withdrawal,
    limited coverage) is the counterfactual the design learned from — which makes the Paris
    regime the field's live experiment in whether ambition can be summoned without
    obligation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The design frontier.</strong> With great-power consensus gone, governance innovation
    runs below the summit: club governance (smaller circles, deeper rules — the WTO's plurilateral
    agreements, mineral security partnerships), polycentric governance (Ostrom's framework — many
    overlapping centres of authority: cities, states, firms and provinces acting on climate in
    parallel), standards bodies (ICAO, ITU, ISO — the quiet machinery that lands planes and
    routes packets), and multi-stakeholder models (ICANN for the internet's naming system being
    the strangest and most durable). The empirical question of the decade: how much global
    coordination can run without hegemonic enforcement?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The AI governance gap.</strong> The newest coordination problem has the oldest
    structure: cross-border effects, racing dynamics, and no institution with authority. Current
    architecture is a patchwork — the EU AI Act (2024, risk-tiered regulation), voluntary
    summits and safety institutes, standards work at ISO and NIST — modelling itself
    self-consciously on nuclear and aviation precedents. Whether it converges into treaty,
    club or standards regime is the field's live experiment in institutional design.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can the Security Council be reformed without being
    destroyed (expansion proposals deadlock on the veto every decade)? Is "effective
    multilateralism" among democracies (alliances of the willing) a complement to the UN or its
    replacement? And what does sovereignty even mean when data, emissions and model weights
    cross borders at zero marginal cost? Each is an active research programme with policy
    consequences this decade.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UN Charter (1945); Paris Agreement (2015); Keohane, After Hegemony (1984); Hale, Held & Young, Gridlock (2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
